Fixed the issue of motherboard loss of control in v3.6 by Bear.

🚀 Solved: Full Control of Skywalker Roaster v3.6 Mainboard with ESP32 (No Level Shifter Needed!)
📌 TL;DR / Executive Summary
If your ESP32-based controller on the new Skywalker v3.6 mainboard exhibits:

Relay clicking but heating elements staying completely dark/cold

Exhaust fan vibrating/stuttering at 60Hz or only running at a weak ~20% speed

Cooling fan running extremely weak

Communication dropping out or watchdog tripping

👉 It is NOT a 3.3V vs 5V logic level voltage problem! You DO NOT need a logic level shifter.

Native 3.3V GPIOs (including onboard USB-C D+/D- on ESP32-S3) drive the v3.6 board reliably.

The real culprit is a firmware timing starvation issue (inter-packet blackout), coupled with an AC Operation Mode lock byte and hardware safety interlocks.

🔍 Detailed Root Cause Analysis
1. The Fatal Bug: Inter-Packet Blackout & TRIAC/PWM Starvation (The 20% Fan Bug)
How the v3.6 Board Works:

Unlike older revisions, the v3.6 mainboard’s onboard MCU requires a continuous, uninterrupted stream of valid control packets to maintain its internal zero-crossing TRIAC phase-angle triggers (exhaust fan and heater) and PWM timers (cooling fan).

The Stock Controller Behavior:

The OEM stock hand controller is a dedicated microcontroller that transmits a ~90ms packet, pauses for barely a few milliseconds, and immediately transmits the next preamble. There is virtually zero transmission downtime.

The Community Firmware Defect:

In previous firmware versions (e.g., v2.37 and earlier), TX packet transmission and RX temperature acquisition were coupled synchronously in the same thread. When calling receiveSerialBitsFromRoaster(), pulseIn() blocks execution for 150ms ~ 240ms waiting for temperature pulses.

The Result:

After sending a 90ms packet, the TX line goes completely silent for 70ms ~ 260ms. During this transmission gap, the roaster MCU’s TRIAC triggers and PWM refresh timers starve and drop out. This causes:

The AC exhaust fan to chatter/vibrate at 60Hz or spin at only ~20% duty cycle.

The heating element TRIAC never achieves sufficient conduction angle (the relay stays closed, but the tube never heats up).

Cooling fan PWM collapses to a crawl.

2. Byte 1: The AC Mode Lock (0x04 vs 0x00)
Byte 1 (Index [1], historically labeled FILTER_BYTE):

On the v3.6 firmware, Byte 1 acts as an AC Power/TRIAC Mode Lock:

During Roasting / Active Venting (VENT > 0 or HEAT > 0): Byte 1 MUST be set to 0x04. Setting it to 0x00 (or 0x01) places the roaster in Standby, locking out the heater TRIAC and corrupting the fan phase triggers.

In Standby / Shutdown: Byte 1 must be set back to 0x00.

3. Safety Interlock (Anti-Dry-Burn Protection)
The v3.6 firmware enforces strict safety rules:

Whenever HEAT > 0, DRUM must be running (100 / 0x64) and exhaust ventilation must be active (VENT >= 50).

If Heat is engaged while Drum is 0, the mainboard’s hardware safety trips the relay within hundreds of milliseconds (causing repetitive 1-second relay clicking).

🛠️ The Solution: Decoupled Continuous TX on Core 0
To eliminate the transmission gaps, TX must be decoupled from temperature reading (pulseIn) and moved to a dedicated FreeRTOS task pinned to Core 0 that streams packets continuously.

1. Continuous Non-Stop TX Function & Task (Pinned to Core 0)
C++
// Buffer definitions
static rmt_symbol_word_t tx_symbols[1 + CONTROLLER_LENGTH * 8];

void sendRoasterMessageContinuous() {
    uint8_t snapshot[CONTROLLER_LENGTH];
    memcpy(snapshot, sendBuffer, CONTROLLER_LENGTH);

    // 🚀 v3.6 Safety & Mode Lock Enforcement
    if (snapshot[VENT_BYTE] > 0 || snapshot[HEAT_BYTE] > 0) {
        snapshot[FILTER_BYTE] = 4;    // Byte 1 MUST be 0x04 to unlock AC TRIACs
        if (snapshot[HEAT_BYTE] > 0) {
            snapshot[DRUM_BYTE] = 100; // Mandatory drum rotation during heat
            if (snapshot[VENT_BYTE] < 50) {
                snapshot[VENT_BYTE] = 50; // Maintain base exhaust airflow
            }
        }
    } else {
        snapshot[FILTER_BYTE] = 0;    // Standby / Cooling mode: 0x00
    }

    // Standard 8-bit checksum: Sum(Byte 0..4) & 0xFF
    uint8_t sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (CONTROLLER_LENGTH - 1); i++) {
        sum += snapshot[i];
    }
    snapshot[CHECK_BYTE] = sum;

    // Encode RMT symbols (Preamble: Low 7500us, High 3800us)
    int idx = 0;
    tx_symbols[idx].val = 0;
    tx_symbols[idx].val |= (START_PULSE & 0x7FFF);
    tx_symbols[idx].val |= (0 << 15);
    tx_symbols[idx].val |= ((START_DELAY & 0x7FFF) << 16);
    tx_symbols[idx].val |= (1U << 31);
    idx++;

    // Data Bits (LSB first: 1 = Low 1500us / 0 = Low 650us, Gap = High 750us)
    for (int i = 0; i < CONTROLLER_LENGTH; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++) {
            int low_dur = (bitRead(snapshot[i], j) == 1) ? 1500 : PULSE_ZERO;
            tx_symbols[idx].val = 0;
            tx_symbols[idx].val |= (low_dur & 0x7FFF);
            tx_symbols[idx].val |= (0 << 15);
            tx_symbols[idx].val |= ((POST_PULSE_DELAY & 0x7FFF) << 16);
            tx_symbols[idx].val |= (1U << 31);
            idx++;
        }
    }

    rmt_transmit_config_t tx_conf = {
        .loop_count = 0,
        .flags = { .eot_level = 1 }
    };

    if (rmt_transmit(tx_channel, copy_encoder, tx_symbols,
                     idx * sizeof(rmt_symbol_word_t), &tx_conf) == ESP_OK) {
        rmt_tx_wait_all_done(tx_channel, 200); // Wait for the ~90ms packet to complete
    }
}

// 🚀 Dedicated Background TX Task: Eliminates the 100ms+ blackout window
void roasterTxTask(void *pvParameters) {
    vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(1000)); // Boot stabilization delay
    for (;;) {
        sendRoasterMessageContinuous();
        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(2)); // Immediately send next packet (2ms yield for WDT)
    }
}
2. Setup Initialization
In setup():

C++
    setupRMT_TX();
    pinMode(RX_PIN, INPUT);

    // Launch background TX task on Core 0 with priority 5
    xTaskCreatePinnedToCore(roasterTxTask, "RoasterTX", 4096, NULL, 5, NULL, 0);
3. Main Loop (loop()) Cleanup
Remove any calls to sendRoasterMessage() inside loop() or receiveSerialBitsFromRoaster(). Let receiveSerialBitsFromRoaster() strictly handle reading incoming 56-bit temperature data without blocking the TX queue.

📊 Verification & Results
Once this firmware structure is applied:

Exhaust Fan: Runs smoothly and whisper-quiet across all power levels (0–100%) without any 60Hz magnetic shuddering.

Heater: The element starts glowing red within 3–5 seconds of engaging heat.

Hardware: Fully verified using a direct USB cable connection (ESP32-S3 Zero / DevKitC-1 straight into the roaster port) without any level shifter hardware.
